{% comment %}
  FUEL_05: the homepage UGC video row.

  This is a snippet, not a section, because the homepage template sits at
  Shopify's 25-section cap and nothing new fits without removing client
  content. It is rendered from inside the "Senaste dofterna" popular-products
  section (sections/popular-products.liquid, gated to that one instance), so
  it takes no template slot. Same approach as the collection-page tiles.

  The seven clips are the client's uploads (six Slack, Aug 26; JoacimE added
  Sep 2). The sources and
  posters below are Shopify's own transcodes of those Files entries, the same
  URLs the product-page slider serves through its video picks. Hard-coded here
  because a snippet has no settings to hold a video pick. If a clip is ever
  re-uploaded, refresh the URLs from the Files entries.

  Every tile carries a product pill (review, Sep 2: "on top of all of the
  videos"). Which product, from the clips themselves:
    1. NathalieE (car): names Valentino Born in Roma, so the women's No. 469.
    2. EmelieS (tattoos): holds the Valentino Uomo Born in Roma bottle next to
       the dupe, so the men's No. 360.
    3. SofiaK (sofa, "fyrpacket är deras bästa erbjudande"): names no scent,
       recommends the four-pack, so the 4x 100 ml bundle.
    4. EbbaW ("den här kostar 174 kronor"): names no scent; the best-selling
       women's scent, Cherry Vanilla No. 438.
    5. SofiaK (unboxing): bottles 469W and 437W on screen, "nummer 437", so
       the women's No. 437.
    6. DannieN (Danish, man): names no scent; the best-selling men's scent,
       Ginger Amber No. 230.
    7. JoacimE (car): holds a Magic Perfume bottle whose label reads 3?0M, the
       middle digit too soft in the 1080p transcode to be sure. Only No. 350 and
       No. 360 fit, so the men's best seller, Uomo Born in Roma No. 360, until the
       client confirms the number.
  Tiles 3, 4 and 6 are fallbacks the client may want to swap; tile 7 needs the
  client to confirm the bottle number.

  The two SofiaK clips (third and fifth below) point at the _sdr Files entries,
  not the originals: those two were recorded as iPhone HLG HDR, every Shopify
  transcode of them kept the HDR tags, and iOS boosts HDR video far past the
  page's white, which read as the clips blowing out the moment they played.
  The _sdr entries are the same footage tone-mapped to BT.709. The collection
  and product templates pick the same _sdr entries.

  SD-480p-1.5Mbps, not the HD-1080p-7.2Mbps file these started on: the card is
  265px wide at most and the 1080p stream had to buffer before a tap could
  produce a frame, which is what read as a dead tap. Each clip has three
  transcodes (SD-480p-1.5Mbps, HD-720p-4.5Mbps, HD-1080p-7.2Mbps) sharing one
  trailing id, so only the rendition name in the middle of the URL differs;
  the 480p files run 5-17MB against 22-77MB. The tile snippet makes the same
  choice on its own for the placements that pass a picked video.

  The seventh clip is the exception: Shopify built it one rendition only,
  HD-1080p-2.5Mbps, and both SD-480p-1.5Mbps and HD-720p-4.5Mbps return 404
  for its id, so the URL below is the 1080p one. Its bitrate is a third of the
  7.2Mbps files the others started on, but if Shopify ever adds the ladder for
  this id, swap the rendition label here the way the other six read.

  Remove this file and its render site with the FUEL_05 cleanup.

  Usage:
  {% render 'fuel05-ugc-home-row' %}
{% endcomment %}

{{ 'fuel05-ugc.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
